    "['local-address [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 update-source Loopback0', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nneighbor 2001:db8:2::2 update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you would typically:\\n1. First configure a loopback interface with the IPv6 address (2001:db8:9::1 in this case)\\n2. Then reference that loopback as the update source in the BGP configuration\\n\\nThe exact loopback interface number isn't specified in the Juniper config, so I used Loopback0 as a common default. The actual implementation would require:\\n```\\ninterface Loopback0\\n ipv6 address 2001:db8:9::1/128\\n!\\nrouter bgp <local-as-number>\\n neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 remote-as 100\\n neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 update-source Loopback0\\n```\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 update-source Loopback0'}]]": 1,
