    "['set policy-options policy-statement [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router bgp 100', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper BGP configuration to Cisco IOS for enabling IPv6 BGP with a neighbor and exporting connected routes:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 remote-as 100\\n neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 update-source Loopback0\\n address-family ipv6\\n  neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 activate\\n  network 2001:db8:9::1/128\\n exit-address-family\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. I assumed the local address (2001:db8:9::1) would be on a Loopback0 interface in Cisco\\n2. The Cisco equivalent of Juniper's policy-statement is implicit - connected routes are automatically advertised when using the 'network' statement for the specific prefix\\n3. The peer-AS is maintained as 100 (though typically in Cisco eBGP would use a different AS)\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 remote-as 100', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translation of the Juniper BGP configuration to Cisco IOS for enabling IPv6 BGP with a neighbor and exporting connected routes:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 remote-as 100\\n neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 update-source Loopback0\\n address-family ipv6\\n  neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 activate\\n  network 2001:db8:9::1/128\\n exit-address-family\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. I assumed the local address (2001:db8:9::1) would be on a Loopback0 interface in Cisco\\n2. The Cisco equivalent of Juniper's policy-statement is implicit - connected routes are automatically advertised when using the 'network' statement for the specific prefix\\n3. The peer-AS is maintained as 100 (though typically in Cisco eBGP would use a different AS)\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 2001:db8:2::2 remote-as 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_comma